.SH "NAME"
(*econf\&.File)\&.HasQuotes \- check whether the value of a key is enclosed by quotes

.SH "SYNOPSIS"

.sp
.ft B
.nf
import "github\&.com/openSUSE/libeconf/go/econf"
.fi
.ft
.sp
.nf
\fBfunc (f *econf\&.File) HasQuotes(group, key string) bool\fR
.fi

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

.PP
\fB(*File)\&.HasQuotes\fR
reports whether the value of the key specified by
\fIgroup\fR
and
\fIkey\fR
in
\fIf\fR
is enclosed by quotes\&. If
\fIgroup\fR
is
""\&, the key is considered to belong to no group\&.

.PP
A value is enclosed by quotes either because it was parsed from a
configuration file where it was written with a leading quote character, or
because it was marked as such with
\fB(*File)\&.SetQuotes\fR
(see
\fBeconf_go_File_Set\fR(3))\&. This flag only affects how the value will be
enclosed by quotes when
\fIf\fR
is written back with
\fB(*File)\&.WriteFile\fR
(see
\fBeconf_go_File_WriteFile\fR(3))\&.

.SH "RETURN VALUE"

.PP
\fBHasQuotes\fR
returns
\fItrue\fR
if the value of the given
\fIgroup\fR/\fIkey\fR
is enclosed by quotes, and
\fIfalse\fR
otherwise\&.

.PP
\fIfalse\fR
is also returned if the
\fIgroup\fR/\fIkey\fR
combination has not been found\&.

.SH "ERRORS"

.PP
This function does not return an error\&.

.SH "EXAMPLE"
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 1
.\}
.nf

f, err := econf\&.ReadFile("/etc/example\&.conf", "=", "#")
if err != nil {
    log\&.Fatal(err)
}
defer f\&.Close()

if f\&.HasQuotes("main", "greeting") {
    fmt\&.Println("greeting is quoted")
}

.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

